\section{System Definition}

A LaTeX Camera is defined as a mapping
\[
\mathcal{C}: \mathcal{S}_{\text{physical}} \to \mathcal{D}_{\text{LaTeX}},
\]
where $\mathcal{S}_{\text{physical}}$ is a physical or simulated state-space
(manifold, fields, tensors) and $\mathcal{D}_{\text{LaTeX}}$ is a structured
document encoding that state in analytic form.

\section{Input State}

The input snapshot state includes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Metric $g_{\mu\nu}$
    \item Curvature tensors $R_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}$, $C_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}$
    \item Gauge fields $A_\mu^a$, $G_{\mu\nu}^a$
    \item Matter fields $\psi$, $\Phi$
    \item Phase-space distribution $f(x^i, v^i, t)$
\end{itemize}

\section{Output Document}

The LaTeX Camera produces:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A title and metadata block
    \item Sections for geometry, gauge content, plasma, and dark-sector
    \item Equations for all active invariants and constraints
    \item Numerical values for key invariants at snapshot time $t = t_\ast$
\end{itemize}

